---
layout: base.njk
# The how-to for what people search: "create H5P with ChatGPT" and the like, where the build page is
# the tool. The description counts the types as the page does, in the release the site runs.
title: "How to create H5P content with ChatGPT, Claude or Grok"
eleventyComputed:
  description: "Step by step: ask ChatGPT, Claude or Grok for an H5P quiz, flash cards or any of {{ skill.offered.length }} H5P types, build the .h5p file free, and add it to Moodle."
socialImage:
  src: /assets/og-h5p-build.png
  width: 1200
  height: 630
  alt: "The build page: its headline, Build an H5P activity with AI, and a course presentation playing beside it."
# The top bar's links to the page's own sections (topbar.njk).
topbarLinks:
  - { href: "#install", text: "Install" }
  - { href: "#prompts", text: "Prompts" }
  - { href: "#types", text: "Types" }
  - { href: "#open-source", text: "Open source" }
  - { href: "#help", text: "Help" }
# No script but the site's: base.njk writes the policy first in <head>, and the color-scheme script
# is added to script-src by its hash at build (eleventy.config.js).
csp: "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'"
---
{#- The prompts are code blocks, which site.js gives a Copy button, named by data-copy-name for a
    screen reader, which hears the page's many alike otherwise. Each carries the skill's page at its
    whole address, which a chat's fetch tool needs. -#}
{%- set skillPage = site.url + "/h5p/skill/" -%}
{% include "topbar.njk" %}

<main class="guide wrap" id="main">
  <header class="docs-header">
    <h1>Create H5P content with ChatGPT, Claude or Grok</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      An AI chat can write a whole H5P activity, not only its questions: a quiz, flash cards, a
      course presentation or any of <a href="#types">{{ skill.offered.length }} H5P types</a>. Here
      is how, from the prompt to the <code>.h5p</code> file in your Moodle.
    </p>
    <p>
      The prompt sends the chat to the h5p-author skill, the instructions that tell it how to write
      H5P. It writes the activity, and <a href="/h5p/build/">the build page</a> makes it a
      <code>.h5p</code> file for Moodle or any other H5P site. Both are free and
      <a href="#open-source">open source</a>, with no account and nothing to install: you need only a
      chat that can open a web page. In Claude or ChatGPT you can also
      <a href="#install">install the skill once</a>.
    </p>
    <p><a class="button button-solid button-large" href="/h5p/build/">Open the build page</a></p>
  </header>

  {#- The steps are drawn as the skill page's timeline (site.css). -#}
  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="ask-heading">
    <h2 id="ask-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">1</span></span> Ask your chat</h2>
    <p>
      Open ChatGPT, Claude, Grok or another AI chat that can open a web page, and paste this prompt
      with your own subject:
    </p>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the prompt">Read {{ skillPage }} and follow it to make an H5P activity on [your subject] for [your students].</pre>
    <p>
      Name the type if you know it, such as "an H5P Question Set of ten questions": the
      <a href="#types">types</a> and <a href="#prompts">prompts to start from</a> are below. For
      another language, say so, such as "in French": the activity's buttons come in it too, where
      H5P has a translation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="answer-heading">
    <h2 id="answer-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">2</span></span> Answer its questions</h2>
    <p>
      If the prompt names no type, the chat proposes one or two that fit your material, and you
      choose. Then it asks whose name and which licence go on the activity, which you may skip, and
      writes it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="file-heading">
    <h2 id="file-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">3</span></span> Get the .h5p file</h2>
    <p>What the chat hands you depends on what it can run:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>a link that opens the activity on the build page;</li>
      <li>its source, a block of JSON: paste it <a href="/h5p/build/#build-it">on the build page</a> and choose Build;</li>
      <li>or the <code>.h5p</code> file itself, from a chat that runs code.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      From a link or a source, the build page builds it in your browser, plays it, and saves the
      file with Download the .h5p.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="check-heading">
    <h2 id="check-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">4</span></span> Play it and check it</h2>
    <p>
      Work through all of it, wrong answers included, and check the answers and feedback against
      your material: the build checks the structure, not whether the content is right.
    </p>
    <p>
      If the build lists problems, choose Copy the problems for the chat and paste them into the
      chat that wrote the source. It fixes them all and writes the source again.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" aria-labelledby="course-heading">
    <h2 id="course-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">5</span></span> Add it to your course</h2>
    <p>
      In Moodle, switch Edit mode on in your course, choose Add an activity or resource, then H5P,
      and upload the file under Package file. The file carries the H5P libraries it needs, but by
      default Moodle installs those it lacks only for a manager or an administrator: if it says a
      library is missing, see <a href="#moodle-libraries">below</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Any other H5P site takes it where it adds H5P content, H5P.com too, and Lumi opens it on your
      computer. With no H5P site, h5p-offline-player plays it
      <a href="https://github.com/missing-elements/h5p-offline-player/blob/main/docs/setup.md">on a site of your own</a>
      or <a href="https://h5p-offline-player.vercel.app/app/">offline, in its app</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  {#- The zips are written by vite.config.ts from the release the site pins, so each holds the skill
      the prompt above sends a chat to. Each was uploaded as it is, by the owner, on 2026-11-13. -#}
  <section aria-labelledby="install">
    <h2 id="install">Or install the skill once</h2>
    <p>
      Installed, the skill stays in your chat: ask for an activity with no address to paste, such as
      "Use the h5p-author skill to make a quiz of five questions on the rivers of Europe".
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Claude</strong>, on claude.ai or in its apps: download
        <a href="/h5p/skill/h5p-author.zip" download>h5p-author.zip</a> and upload it under Customize,
        then Skills, with code execution on. Claude builds the <code>.h5p</code> file in the chat.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>ChatGPT</strong>: download
        <a href="/h5p/skill/h5p-author-chatgpt.zip" download>h5p-author-chatgpt.zip</a> and upload it
        under Plugins. ChatGPT cannot install the build tool, so it hands over a link to the build
        page. If the page says the link arrived damaged, ask for the link again, or for the source
        as a file, and open that on the build page.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Open WebUI</strong>: an administrator imports the skill under Import from URL, from
        its folder on GitHub,
        <code>https://github.com/missing-elements/h5p-offline-player/tree/main/skills/h5p-author</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An agent with a terminal</strong>, such as Claude Code, Codex or Cursor:
        <code>npx skills add missing-elements/h5p-offline-player --skill h5p-author</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>The zips hold h5p-author {{ skill.version }}, the release the build page runs.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="prompts">
    <h2 id="prompts">Prompts to start from</h2>
    <p>
      Each names its type, so the chat asks only whose name and licence go on it. Change the subject
      and the numbers to yours.
    </p>

    <h3>A course presentation</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the course presentation prompt">Read {{ skillPage }} and follow it to make an H5P Course Presentation of six slides on writing learning objectives with Bloom's taxonomy, with a question on each slide.</pre>
    <p>
      Claude Haiku made the sample on the build page from this prompt:
      <a href="/h5p/build/">play it there</a>, or <a href="/h5p/build/sample.h5p" download>download it</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>A quiz</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the quiz prompt">Read {{ skillPage }} and follow it to make an H5P Question Set of ten multiple-choice questions on the water cycle for 11-year-olds, with feedback that explains each answer.</pre>

    <h3>Flash cards</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the flash cards prompt">Read {{ skillPage }} and follow it to make H5P Dialog Cards for twenty common Spanish verbs: the verb on the front, and its meaning and an example sentence on the back.</pre>

    <h3>Fill in the blanks, from your text</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the fill in the blanks prompt">Read {{ skillPage }} and follow it to make an H5P Fill in the Blanks from the text below, with its key terms as the blanks.

[Paste your text here]</pre>

    <h3>An interactive book, from your notes</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the interactive book prompt">Read {{ skillPage }} and follow it to make an H5P Interactive Book from my lesson notes below: a chapter for each topic, each ending with two questions.

[Paste your notes here]</pre>

    <h3>A branching scenario</h3>
    <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt" data-copy-name="the branching scenario prompt">Read {{ skillPage }} and follow it to make an H5P Branching Scenario in which a new team lead deals with a project running late: three decisions, each with a better and a worse choice, and feedback at every end.</pre>
  </section>

  {#- The types the skill's table offers, in the release the site runs, by their names in H5P's
      editor (src/_data/skill.js): those the chat writes whole, then those that play the user's
      media. -#}
  <section aria-labelledby="types">
    <h2 id="types">The {{ skill.offered.length }} H5P content types it offers</h2>
    <p>
      The chat offers these, from the h5p-author skill's list. Name one in your prompt, or say what
      you want and let it propose one.
    </p>

    <h3>From text alone</h3>
    <dl class="type-list">
      {%- for type in skill.offered | rejectattr("media") %}
      <div>
        <dt>{{ type.title }}</dt>
        <dd>{{ type.use }}.</dd>
      </div>
      {%- endfor %}
    </dl>

    <h3>With your pictures, video or recordings</h3>
    <p>
      Attach your files to the chat, or tell it their names, and it writes the activity around
      them. The build page then asks you to choose them from your computer.
    </p>
    <dl class="type-list">
      {%- for type in skill.offered | selectattr("media") %}
      <div>
        <dt>{{ type.title }}</dt>
        <dd>{{ type.use }}. <span class="type-media">You add {{ type.media }}.</span></dd>
      </div>
      {%- endfor %}
    </dl>
  </section>

  {#- What most people ask first, which the header answers in a word and this section in full. -#}
  <section aria-labelledby="open-source">
    <h2 id="open-source">Free and open source</h2>
    <p>
      The build page and the h5p-author skill are free, need no account, and are open source under
      the MIT licence. The build page's code is in
      <a href="https://github.com/missing-elements/missing-elements.github.io/tree/main/src/h5p/build">this site's repository</a>,
      and the skill and the player are in
      <a href="https://github.com/missing-elements/h5p-offline-player">h5p-offline-player</a>. The H5P
      runtime the player loads is <a href="/h5p/assets/runtime-LICENSE.txt">GPL-3.0</a>, and the H5P
      libraries in a file are each under their own licence, most MIT, as
      <a href="/h5p/assets/libraries-LICENSES.txt">the pack's licence list</a> says.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing you make is sent to this site: the build page builds and plays it in your browser.
      Your AI chat sees what you send it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="help">
    <h2 id="help">If something goes wrong</h2>

    <h3>The chat can't open the address</h3>
    <p>
      Turn on its web search or browsing, if it has one, and send the prompt again. The address has
      to be whole, <code>https://</code> and all, as in the prompts here.
    </p>

    <h3>The build lists problems</h3>
    <p>
      Choose Copy the problems for the chat, and paste them into the chat that wrote the source. It
      fixes them all and writes the source again: paste that in place of the old one, and build
      again.
    </p>

    <h3>The chat's link builds nothing</h3>
    <p>
      Ask the chat for the source as JSON instead, and paste it on the build page. A link is made by
      running code, and one a chat wrote out itself breaks.
    </p>

    <h3 id="moodle-libraries">Moodle says a library is missing</h3>
    <p>
      The file carries every library it needs, but by default Moodle lets only managers and
      administrators install new ones. Ask one to upload the file in Site administration, under
      Manage H5P content types: after that, files with the same types work for you too.
    </p>
  </section>

</main>

